  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework's performance and the generalizability of the observed traffic discrepancies were not validated against independent, ground-truth traffic sensor datasets in the abstract.",
      "what": "The study introduces a three-layer framework comprising data, interactive software, and processing method layers to estimate urban traffic patterns from car-hailing GPS trace data. The system processes raw traces through data cutting, map matching, and trace correction to generate matrices of vehicle flow and average road speeds. Analysis using this framework revealed that urban road traffic follows a bimodal temporal pattern, whereas car-hailing flow displays a multipeak pattern with high holiday fluctuations, though both share similar spatial hotspot distributions.",
      "why": "It establishes a systematic method to correct and map car-hailing GPS traces, revealing distinct temporal differences between ride-hailing flows and general urban traffic that planners must account for when using commercial fleet data as a proxy for overall traffic."
    }
  },
